# Environment template.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored and must stay that way — it
# holds a database password and an API key.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every variable here is declared in src/lib/env.ts, which parses this file's
# values once at server start. A missing or malformed one stops the server with
# a list of what is wrong, so there is no such thing as a half-configured boot.
# Adding a variable means adding it to that schema and to this file.

# ─── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Postgres, used by Prisma and the app. Must be a postgres:// or postgresql://
# URL. The database has to exist already; `npm run db:push` creates the tables.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/church?schema=public"

# ─── Recommended ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where this instance is reachable, used to build the absolute links inside
# invitation and password-reset emails. Defaults to http://localhost:3000.
#
# Set it correctly before inviting anyone: the link in their inbox is built
# from this, so a stale value sends real people to a host they cannot reach.
#
# In production this must be https:// unless it points at localhost — session
# cookies are set `secure`, and over plain http to a real host they never come
# back, which looks like everybody being signed out at random.
APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ─── Email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Resend — https://resend.com/api-keys
#
# Optional everywhere. Leave it empty and outbound mail is printed to the
# server log instead of sent, so you can pick invitation and reset links out of
# the terminal without registering for anything — and so a deployment can be
# stood up before email is wired to it.
#
# Set it before inviting anyone real: without it, an invitation or a password
# reset goes to the log rather than to the member. The server warns loudly at
# boot when it is missing in production.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# The sender. Must be an address on a domain verified in Resend. Plain address
# or "Display Name <address@domain>" — both are accepted.
EMAIL_FROM="Church <noreply@example.com>"

# ─── First administrator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Read once on server start, and only when the users table contains no admin at
# all — after that the account exists and these are ignored.
#
# Remove them from the environment once you have signed in. The running system
# does not need them, and a password sitting in the environment of a live
# server is worth more to an attacker than the convenience is worth to you.
#
# The password must be at least 12 characters. The account is created with
# "must change password" set, so it has to be replaced at first sign-in.
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@example.org"
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=""
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_NAME="Church Office"

# ─── File storage (Cloudflare R2) ───────────────────────────────────────────

# Sermon audio, handout PDFs and avatars. Optional: leave all five empty and
# the portal runs with uploads switched off (YouTube links still work). Set
# all five or none — a partial set stops the server with a list of what is
# missing.
#
# In the Cloudflare dashboard:
#   1. R2 → Create bucket (for example "kmmi-media").
#   2. Bucket → Settings → Public access: enable the r2.dev URL or connect a
#      custom domain. That address is R2_PUBLIC_URL.
#   3. Bucket → Settings → CORS policy, so browsers can upload straight to it:
#        [{ "AllowedOrigins": ["https://your-portal.example"],
#           "AllowedMethods": ["PUT"], "AllowedHeaders": ["content-type"],
#           "MaxAgeSeconds": 3600 }]
#      (add http://localhost:3000 while developing)
#   4. R2 → Manage API tokens → Create token with "Object Read & Write" on
#      that bucket. It shows the access key id and secret once.
#   5. The account id is in the R2 overview page's sidebar.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=""
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
R2_BUCKET=""
R2_PUBLIC_URL=""

# ─── Scheduled jobs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The secret Vercel Cron sends to /api/cron/daily and /api/cron/reminders
# (see vercel.json). At least 16 characters; generate one with
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Set the same value as CRON_SECRET in the Vercel project, which sends it as
# "Authorization: Bearer <secret>". Leave it empty and the jobs refuse every
# call: no birthday, devotion, pledge or event reminders are sent.
CRON_SECRET=""

# ─── Build ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Not set in .env. Set it in CI or a Docker build stage, where `next build`
# imports every module — including the env schema — on a machine that has no
# business holding production secrets:
#
#   SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION=1 npm run build
#
# It substitutes obvious placeholders for the build only. The server re-parses
# for real at boot, so this cannot mask a misconfigured deployment.
